  "description": "Original study: Andreoni, J., & Miller, J. H. (1993). Rational cooperation in the finitely repeated prisoner's dilemma: Experimental evidence. The Economic Journal, 103(418), 570-585. Pairs played a prisoner's dilemma for a known, fixed number of rounds; the study compared fixed partners with re-matched strangers to test whether people cooperate early in a finitely repeated game to build a reputation. Prisoner's dilemma games appear in 43 of the 500 studies in our survey. This template runs one ten-round game with a fixed partner (the partners design), with payoffs of 7 each for mutual cooperation, 4 each for mutual defection, and 12 and 0 when one defects; the original repeated such games many times. Participants are simulated: each is a Model participant, using the free test model until you choose a model, so the first runs only check the design; with the test model every participant takes the sample answer.",
